    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Congress voted for independence on 2 July 1776 and approved the wording of the Declaration on 4 July; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a> wrote most of the first draft, and Congress cut nearly a fourth of it.</li><li>It gave the political, moral and legal grounds for independence, citing 27 grievances against King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a> and asserting natural and legal rights, including a right of revolution.</li><li>Little noticed in the years after the Revolution, it gained a second life from the 1790s and was later invoked by abolitionists, women's rights campaigners, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln">Abraham Lincoln</a> and many new states.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Jefferson's draft charged King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a> with forcing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> on the colonies, but Congress struck the passage out, in part to appease <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, which had significant involvement in the slave trade. Jefferson later wrote that Congress had 'mangled' his draft.</p></aside>
    </section>

