    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1775</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a> begins in April with the battles of Lexington and Concord, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-continental-congress">Second Continental Congress</a> meets in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> in May</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1776</span><span class="rf-text">On 11 June Congress appoints the Committee of Five, and Jefferson writes the first draft between 11 and 28 June</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1776</span><span class="rf-text">On 2 July Congress adopts the resolution of independence, and on 4 July it approves the final wording of the Declaration and sends it to the printer</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1776</span><span class="rf-text">John Dunlap's broadsides are read in public on 8 July in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>, Trenton and Easton, and most delegates sign the engrossed copy on 2 August</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1823</span><span class="rf-text">A facsimile of the engrossed copy is made, and it becomes the basis of most modern reproductions</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1848</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-falls-convention">Seneca Falls Convention</a> issues a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/declaration-of-sentiments">Declaration of Sentiments</a> patterned on the Declaration, with the words 'all men and women are created equal'</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1863</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln">Abraham Lincoln</a> makes the Declaration the centrepiece of his Gettysburg Address</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1952</span><span class="rf-text">The engrossed Declaration is transferred to the National Archives, where it is on permanent display</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson.jpg" alt="Thomas Jefferson" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Official_Presidential_portrait_of_Thomas_Jefferson_(by_Rembrandt_Peale,_1800).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rembrandt Peale, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By July 1776 the Thirteen Colonies and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> had been at war for over a year. Relations had been worsening since 1763, and Parliament's measures to raise revenue, including the Stamp Act of 1765 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/townshend-acts">Townshend Acts</a>, met the argument that Parliament, in which the colonies were not directly represented, had no right to tax them. The orthodox British view, dating from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution-spain">Glorious Revolution</a> of 1688, held Parliament supreme throughout the empire, while in the colonies the idea had grown that the British Constitution recognised fundamental rights no government could violate. By 1774 writers such as Samuel Adams, James Wilson and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a> argued that Parliament was the legislature of Great Britain only.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/intolerable-acts">Coercive Acts</a> of 1774, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-continental-congress">First Continental Congress</a> organised a boycott and petitioned the king. The war began in April 1775, yet most colonists still hoped for reconciliation until the king rejected a second petition and issued a Proclamation of Rebellion in 1775. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-paine">Thomas Paine</a>'s Common Sense appeared in January 1776 and opened a public debate on independence. Congress could not act at will: delegates were bound by instructions from their colonies, and between April and July 1776 a 'complex political war' was waged to have these revised. Historian Pauline Maier counts more than ninety local declarations of independence in those months.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;United States Declaration of Independence&#8221; cites where it covers Background, Continental Congress convenes, Growing support for independence, Revising instructions:</p><ul><li>Merrill Jensen, The Founding of a Nation: A History of the American Revolution, 1763–1776 (Oxford University Press, 1968)</li><li>Herbert Friedenwald, The Declaration of Independence: An Interpretation and an Analysis (The Macmillan Company, 1904)</li><li>Robert Middlekauff, The Glorious Cause: The American Revolution, 1763–1789 (Oxford University Press, 2005)</li><li>Bernard Bailyn, The Ideological Origins of the American Revolution (Harvard University Press, 1992)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="content">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/george-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-iii.jpg" alt="George III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Allan_Ramsay_-_King_George_III_in_coronation_robes_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Allan Ramsay, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Declaration is not divided into formal sections, but it is often discussed as five parts: an introduction, a preamble, an indictment of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a>, a denunciation of the British people and a conclusion. It justified the independence of the colonies by citing 27 grievances against the king and by asserting certain natural and legal rights, including a right of revolution. Its second sentence holds that 'all men are created equal' and are endowed by their Creator with unalienable rights, among them 'Life, Liberty and the pursuit of Happiness'.</p><p>Historians disagree about what kind of document it is. John Phillip Reid argued that it is less a philosophical tract on natural rights than a legal indictment of the king for violating the constitutional rights of the colonists. David Armitage argued that its primary purpose was to express the international legal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, since the revolutionaries had to show they were no longer dependent on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> if European powers were to recognise them. The Declaration does not have the force of law domestically, but it may help to provide historical and legal clarity about the Constitution and other laws.</p><p>Jefferson's own draft included a passage condemning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> and blaming King George III for it. Congress deleted it, so it does not appear in the final text.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="making">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Was Made</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-adams"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-adams.jpg" alt="John Adams" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/john-adams">John Adams</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gilbert_Stuart,_John_Adams,_c._1800-1815,_NGA_42933.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gilbert Stuart, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>On 7 June 1776 Richard Henry Lee, following instructions from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> Convention, presented a resolution that the colonies 'are, and of right ought to be, free and independent States'. Congress postponed the debate for three weeks and, on 11 June, appointed the Committee of Five: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-adams">John Adams</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-franklin">Benjamin Franklin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a>, Robert R. Livingston and Roger Sherman. The committee took no minutes, and the accounts later written by Jefferson and Adams contradict each other. What is certain is that Adams persuaded the committee to choose Jefferson as the writer. Jefferson largely wrote the draft between 11 and 28 June in a rented house at 700 Market Street in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>, and the committee presented its copy to Congress on 28 June.</p><p>His most immediate sources were his own draft of the preamble of the Constitution of Virginia and George Mason's draft of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia-declaration-of-rights">Virginia Declaration of Rights</a>. Scholars disagree about wider influences: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-locke">John Locke</a> is usually cited, but others stress Jean Jacques Burlamaqui, Francis Hutcheson, republicanism or de Vattel's The Law of Nations. Jefferson himself wrote in 1825 that it was not aiming at originality and was meant to be 'an expression of the American mind'.</p><p>Congress edited the draft for two days, shortening it by a fourth. It voted on independence on 1 July, when nine delegations voted yes, and on 2 July, when twelve voted yes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> abstained. It approved the final wording on 4 July.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="reception">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Reception</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/george-washington"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-washington.jpg" alt="George Washington" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gilbert_Stuart_Williamstown_Portrait_of_George_Washington.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gilbert Stuart, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>John Dunlap printed about 200 broadsides on the night of 4 July. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> Evening Post published the text on 6 July, and it was read publicly on 8 July at noon in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>, Trenton and Easton. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a> had it read to his troops in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> City on 9 July, and in many cities crowds tore down signs and statues of royal authority; a statue of King George in New York City was pulled down and its lead made into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musket</a> balls. The text reached British newspapers from mid-August, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> by mid-September, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in November 1776.</p><p>The North Ministry gave no official answer, but secretly commissioned John Lind to publish an Answer to the Declaration of the American Congress, and Thomas Hutchinson, the former royal governor of Massachusetts, also published a rebuttal. Hutchinson argued that the Revolution was the work of a few conspirators. Lind's pamphlet carried an attack on natural rights by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeremy-bentham">Jeremy Bentham</a>. Both asked how slaveholders in Congress could proclaim that all men are created equal without freeing their own slaves.</p><p>In late November 1776 a group of 547 Loyalists, largely from New York, signed a Declaration of Dependence pledging loyalty to the Crown. The Spanish-American authorities banned the Declaration, but it was widely translated and passed on.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;United States Declaration of Independence&#8221; cites where it covers Influences and legal status, Global influence:</p><ul><li>David Armitage, The Declaration of Independence: A Global History (Harvard University Press, 2007)</li><li>Julian P. Boyd, The Declaration of Independence: The Evolution of the Text as Shown in Facsimiles of Various Drafts by Its Author, Thomas Jefferson (Princeton University Press, 1945)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="survival">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Survival &amp; Rediscovery</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/magna-carta"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/magna-carta.jpg" alt="Magna Carta" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/magna-carta">Magna Carta</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Magna_Carta_(British_Library_Cotton_MS_Augustus_II.106).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Original authors were the barons and King John of England. Uploaded by Earthsound., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Delegates knew that signing was an act of high treason, which was punishable by torture and death. Congress resolved on 19 July to have the Declaration engrossed on parchment under the title 'The unanimous declaration of the thirteen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> of America'. The clerk Timothy Matlack probably engrossed it, and most delegates signed it on 2 August 1776; fifty-six signatures are affixed. Thomas McKean disputed in 1796 that it was signed on 4 July, and historians have generally accepted his version. The Dunlap broadside is also regarded as an official text by Julian P. Boyd, who argued that the Declaration, like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magna-carta">Magna Carta</a>, is not a single document. Of about 200 broadsides printed, 26 are known to survive, the last found in The National Archives in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 2009.</p><p>The engrossed copy was poorly conserved in the 19th century, and much of its ink had faded by 1876; a facsimile of 1823 became the basis of most modern reproductions. Custody passed from the State <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> to the Library of Congress in 1921. After the Japanese attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attack-on-pearl-harbor">Pearl Harbor</a> in 1941 it was moved to Fort Knox until 1944, and in 1952 it went to the National Archives. A second engrossed copy, the 'Sussex Declaration', was announced in 2017 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-sussex">West Sussex</a>, England; how it got there is not yet known. Jefferson's 'original Rough draught' survives, but the first draft and the copy submitted to Congress on 28 June were lost.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/william-lloyd-garrison"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-lloyd-garrison.jpg" alt="William Lloyd Garrison" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/william-lloyd-garrison">William Lloyd Garrison</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_Lloyd_Garrison,_abolitionist,_journalist,_and_editor_of_The_Liberator_LCCN2017660623_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Liljenquist Family Collection of Civil War Photographs, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Declaration received little attention in the years after the Revolution: early Independence Day celebrations and histories largely ignored it, and it was rarely mentioned in the debates over the Constitution. Interest revived in the 1790s, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republican-party">Jeffersonian Republicans</a> promoted it and Jefferson as its author while Federalists emphasised the whole Congress. After the War of 1812 it gained a wider following, and from the 1820s variations of it were issued to proclaim the rights of workers, farmers and women, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-falls-convention">Seneca Falls Convention</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/declaration-of-sentiments">Declaration of Sentiments</a> of 1848.</p><p>Its relation to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> was debated from the start. Abolitionists such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-lloyd-garrison">William Lloyd Garrison</a> treated it as a founding text alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>, while defenders of slavery such as John C. Calhoun argued that 'all men are created equal' was false or did not apply to black people. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln">Abraham Lincoln</a> read it as setting a standard for all men, as in his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoria">Peoria</a> speech of 1854 and the Gettysburg Address of 1863; Pauline Maier judged Stephen Douglas's narrower reading more historically accurate, but Lincoln's view prevailed.</p><p>Abroad, the French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/declaration-of-the-rights-of-man-and-of-the-citizen">Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen</a> (1789) drew on it, and David Armitage argues that it began a genre of declarations of independence, whose later examples include Venezuela (1811), Liberia (1847) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> (1945).</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;United States Declaration of Independence&#8221; cites where it covers Legacy, Revival of interest, John Trumbull's Declaration of Independence (1817–1826), Slavery and the Declaration:</p><ul><li>David Armitage, The Declaration of Independence: A Global History (Harvard University Press, 2007)</li><li>John Brown, A Declaration of Liberty By the Representatives of the slave Popolation of the United States of America (Historical Society of Pennsylvania, 1859)</li></ul></details>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Declaration of Independence explained why the Thirteen Colonies saw themselves as independent states, listing 27 grievances against King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a>. Adopted on 4 July 1776 and drafted largely by Jefferson, it was little noticed at first, then became a standard for movements and new states.</em></p>

